            {"heading": "Why This Course Starts With Psychology, Not Math",
             "body": "Most personal finance classes start with math: budgets, interest rates, spreadsheets. This one starts somewhere else, with how you think. Research on why people struggle with money keeps landing on the same finding: most money problems aren't caused by not knowing enough math. They're caused by decisions made under stress, habit, pressure, or emotion. You can know exactly how compound interest works and still make a purchase five minutes later that has nothing to do with math. Before this course gets into spending plans, credit, and taxes, you're going to spend two weeks looking at the thinking patterns that drive every one of those decisions."},
            {"heading": "What Is Financial Thinking?",
             "body": "Financial thinking is the pattern of beliefs, habits, and automatic reactions you bring to any decision involving money. It's shaped by what you watched your family do growing up, what your friend group treats as normal, what ads and social media tell you is worth having, and what's happened to you personally, like a time you got burned by a bad purchase or felt proud of something you saved for. Nobody starts from zero. You already have financial thinking, whether or not you've ever named it. This unit is about noticing it, not judging it, so you can decide which parts are actually serving you."},
