            {"heading": "From Vague Wish to Real Goal",
             "body": "So far this unit has focused mostly on the forces that pull your money decisions off course: bias, FOMO, peer pressure. Today shifts to a tool that helps you stay on course: the S.M.A.R.T. goal framework. Most people's financial goals start out vague, \"save more,\" \"stop spending so much,\" and vague goals are almost impossible to actually reach because there's no clear finish line and no way to check progress along the way. S.M.A.R.T. goals fix that by forcing a goal to answer five specific questions."},
            {"heading": "The Five Parts of a S.M.A.R.T. Goal",
             "body": "S.M.A.R.T. stands for Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. Specific means naming exactly what you want, not a general direction. Measurable means attaching a number so you can track progress. Achievable means it's realistic given your actual income and time. Relevant means it connects to something that actually matters to you, not just something you think you should want. Time-bound means it has a deadline, because a goal without a deadline tends to quietly disappear."},
            {"heading": "A Worked Example",
             "body": "Here's the shift in action. Vague goal: \"I want to save money for college.\" S.M.A.R.T. version: \"I will save $50 a month from my part-time job into a separate savings account, reaching $600 by the end of this school year, to help cover a technical certification deposit.\" Notice every part of S.M.A.R.T. shows up: a number, a timeline, a real reason, and something realistic given a part-time paycheck."},
