            {"heading": "What Is Advertising Influence?",
             "body": "Advertising influence is the effect that marketing, sponsored content, and promotional messaging has on your wants and decisions, often without you noticing it's happening. Good advertising doesn't feel like advertising; it feels like discovering something on your own. Companies research exactly which colors, words, and messengers make teenagers more likely to buy, and they test those choices constantly. Recognizing advertising influence doesn't mean every ad is manipulative or that wanting something you saw in an ad is wrong. It means knowing when an emotional reaction is being deliberately engineered instead of naturally occurring."},
            {"heading": "Evaluating Source Information and Credibility",
             "body": "Source information and credibility means being able to judge whether information about money, or about a product, actually deserves your trust. A credible source is transparent about who created it and what, if anything, they gain from you believing it. A company blog telling you why you need their product is a different kind of source than an independent consumer guide comparing several options. Before trusting a financial claim or a glowing review, it helps to ask three questions: Who wrote this? What do they get if I believe it? Can I find the same claim somewhere with no stake in my answer?"},
